<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="polyphosphate metabolism" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Polyphosphates are linear polymers containing a few to several hundred residues of orthophosphate linked by energy-rich phosphoanhydride bonds. They occur in representatives of all kingdoms of living organisms. One major role of polyphsphates is as a storage form for phosphate. When phosphate is abundant, organisms can form polyphosphate from ATP by the action of |FRAME: EC-2.7.4.1|. When conditions change and posphate becomes limiting, phosphate can be liberated from the polymers with no expenditure of energy by |FRAME: EC-3.6.1.11|. In addition to serving as a phosphorus and energy source, polyphosphates have been shown to be involved in other processes |CITS: [16232585]|, including: &lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Regulation of the levels of ATP and other nucleotide and nucleoside-containing coenzymes.&lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Participation in the regulation of homeostasis and storage of inorganic cations and other positively charged solutes in an osmotically inert form.&lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Formation of channels across the membrane.&lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Participation in the formation and functions of cell surface structures.&lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Control of gene activity.&lt;br&gt; &amp;bull; Regulation of activities of the enzymes and enzyme assemblies involved in the metabolism of nucleic acids and other acid biopolymers In humans polyphosphates also play a key role in blood coagulation. Produced and released by platelets, they activate |FRAME: Factor-XII &quot;factor XII&quot;|, which is essential for blood clot formation |CITS: [15308650][20005807]|.</Description>
